{"entity": "Resistor", "type": "artifact", "description": "Resistor is a passive two-terminal electrical component that implements electrical resistance as a fundamental circuit element. It is consistently labeled “R” (and occasionally “R/2”) in schematic diagrams, appearing in figures such as 4.1 and 4.2, and is represented graphically either by a zigzag line symbol or, in some contexts, a rectangular block—both denoting identical functional behavior. As a passive device, it does not generate energy but instead dissipates electrical energy as heat when current flows through it, fulfilling essential roles in current regulation, voltage division, biasing, signal conditioning, and establishing voltage drops (denoted URx). Resistors are used across diverse configurations—including series, parallel, and hybrid arrangements—and are central to foundational circuit analysis experiments, such as first-order RC circuits, where a resistor is connected in parallel with a capacitor between output terminals to illustrate transient and steady-state responses.\n\nThe resistor’s behavior is frequency-independent under direct current (DC) conditions and exhibits resistive dominance in alternating current (AC) applications, though its impedance and voltage–current relationship can be experimentally measured in AC contexts—for instance, with a specified resistance value of 1 kΩ. Resistance values vary widely across practical implementations; for example, a 100-ohm resistor is commonly inserted in series with other circuit elements to enable indirect current measurement via the associated voltage drop. Moreover, measurement accuracy is sensitive to instrument placement: meter positioning introduces variability in observed voltage and current distributions, reflecting real-world experimental error. Resistors thus serve both theoretical and applied functions—as standardized schematic symbols, quantifiable physical components with defined ohmic values, and critical variables in experimental pedagogy involving signal generators, oscilloscopes, and circuit characterization.<SEP>Resistors with values of 1kΩ each are used in the experimental circuit to form branches and enable voltage and current measurements."}
{"entity": "Ammeter", "type": "artifact", "description": "The ammeter is an electrical measurement instrument specifically designed to measure electric current, denoted as I or Iₓ, flowing through a circuit. It is always connected in series with the component or branch whose current is being measured—such as a main 200 kΩ resistor, a load resistor Rₗ, an equivalent resistance Rₑq, or the entire series RLC circuit—ensuring that the same current passes through both the component and the instrument. In circuit diagrams, the ammeter is conventionally represented by a circular symbol labeled “A”, often positioned near the top center of the schematic to indicate its role in measuring total circuit current. Its function is central to experimental learning objectives involving current measurement and circuit analysis. Importantly, although idealized models assume zero internal resistance, real-world ammeters possess non-zero internal resistance, which introduces measurable error into current readings—a critical practical consideration when interpreting results or designing precise measurement setups.<SEP>The ammeter is an instrument used to measure current, with an internal resistance of 0.2 Ω, whose placement relative to the voltmeter influences total measurement error."}
